    <h2>The Crack You See Started in the Dirt You Don't</h2>

    <p>Concrete is strong in compression and weak in tension, which is a technical way of saying it handles weight pressing down but tears apart when the ground beneath it moves. The Shoals sits on the kind of clay-heavy soil that moves a lot — it holds water, swells in a wet North Alabama spring, and shrinks back in a dry August. A slab poured on that clay without a proper compacted base is riding a surface that lifts and drops with the seasons. What you get is settling cracks, one corner sitting lower than the rest, and a driveway that looked perfect the week it was finished. The concrete didn't fail. The dirt did.</p>

    <h2>Why Pouring It Thicker Solves Nothing</h2>

    <p>The instinct when a slab cracks is to assume the next one needs more concrete — pour it six inches instead of four, add rebar, call it done. That misses the actual problem. A thicker slab on an unstable base still moves with the base; it just cracks in bigger, more expensive pieces. Rebar and wire mesh hold a cracked slab together so it doesn't separate and trip you, but they don't stop the crack from forming. The failure here is spending more money reinforcing the top while ignoring the bottom. A crew that quotes a thicker pour without a word about the subgrade is selling you concrete, not a solution.</p>

    <h2>The Base Is the Whole Job</h2>

    <p>Here's the part good concrete contractors obsess over and mediocre ones rush: what happens before the truck shows up. Proper prep on Shoals clay means stripping the soft topsoil, bringing in and compacting a crushed-stone base that drains instead of holding water against the slab, and grading it so water runs away from the pour and away from the house. Control joints get cut into the fresh slab within about 24 hours, spaced so the concrete cracks where you want it to — in a straight tooled line — instead of randomly across the middle. That's the honest truth of the trade: some cracking is unavoidable, so the skill is controlling where it happens, not pretending it won't. On the older sidewalks and foundation flatwork around East Florence and the Sheffield historic districts, that base work matters even more, because the original pours are a century into riding that same clay.</p>

    <h2>What Good Flatwork Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Driveways and patios</strong> — about $8 to $15 per square foot installed, before tear-out or heavy base repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Standard two-car driveway</strong> — roughly $4,500 to $9,000 depending on size and grade.</li>
      <li><strong>Walkways and small repairs</strong> — starting around $1,200.</li>
      <li><strong>Tear-out and re-pour of a failed slab</strong> — a real add, because hauling off the old concrete and rebuilding the base is most of the labor.</li>
    </ul>

    <p id="pricing-summary">The number swings hardest on base condition. A new Muscle Shoals driveway on already-graded subdivision fill is straightforward; a replacement in an older Florence or Sheffield yard where the original base was never done right costs more, because someone finally has to do it right.</p>

    <h2>When a Slab Has Told You It's Done</h2>

    <p>A hairline crack you can barely catch a fingernail in is normal and not worth worrying about. A crack wide enough to turn an ankle, or one side of a slab sitting noticeably lower than the other, is structural movement that only gets worse. And any slab pushing water toward your foundation instead of away from it is a problem worth fixing before the water finds the house — regrading a bad pour is cheaper than what water does to a crawlspace over a few winters.</p>

